{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"type":"text","text":"This session is being continued from a previous conversation that ran out of context. The summary below covers the earlier portion of the conversation.\n\nSummary:\n1. Primary Request and Intent:\n   The user is executing task #5172 from the cobbler code generation pipeline. The task is to modify `cmd/seq/main.go` to implement four specific SRD requirements:\n   - **srd019-seq R2.4**: `-w` flag pads output numbers with leading zeros for equal width, determined by the widest endpoint string representation\n   - **srd019-seq R3.1**: When arguments contain decimal points, preserve maximum decimal precision from all arguments\n   - **srd019-seq R3.2**: When all arguments are integers (no decimal point), output as integers with no decimal point unless `-f` overrides\n   - **srd019-seq R3.3**: Negative numbers and negative increments work correctly for descending sequences\n\n   Six acceptance criteria must pass: AC1 (`seq -w 1 10` → zero-padded 01-10), AC2 (`seq -w 5 -1 1` → descending same-width), AC3 (`seq 0.5 0.5 2.0` → preserves decimal), AC4 (`seq 5` → integer output), AC5 (`seq 5 -1 1` → descending), AC6 (`go vet` passes).\n\n2. Key Technical Concepts:\n   - GNU seq implementation with differential testing against `gseq` reference binary\n   - Equal-width zero-padding via `-w` flag using `fmt.Sprintf` with `%0Nd` or `%0N.Mf` format\n   - Floating-point precision detection from argument string representations\n   - Integer vs float output format selection based on argument form\n   - Design Decision D1: Width determined from formatted FIRST and LAST string representations\n   - Design Decision D2: `-f` takes precedence over `-w` when both specified\n   - SIGPIPE handler requirement for all cmd/ utilities\n   - Go module at `github.com/petar-djukic/go-unix-utils`\n\n3. Files and Code Sections:\n   - **`cmd/seq/main.go`** (existing, 346 lines) - The file to modify. Already implements:\n     - Flag parsing for `-f`, `-s`, `-w` (but `-w` effect NOT implemented in output)\n     - `parseArgs()` - argument/flag parsing with short and long flag support\n     - `parseOperands()` - handles 1, 2, or 3 argument forms for FIRST/INCR/LAST\n     - `parseNumber()` - validates and parses float64 from string\n     - `validateFormat()` / `scanConversion()` - validates printf format strings\n     - `printSequence()` - the main output loop (needs `-w` support added)\n     - `inRange()` - checks if value is within sequence bounds (handles negative incr)\n     - `defaultFormat()` - computes format string from argument precision (returns `\"%.0f\"` for integers, `\"%.Nf\"` for floats)\n     - `argPrecision()` - counts decimal digits in argument string\n     \n     Key existing code for `printSequence`:\n     ```go\n     func printSequence(opts options, first, incr, last float64, rawArgs []string) {\n         w := bufio.NewWriter(os.Stdout)\n         defer w.Flush()\n         format := opts.format\n         if format == \"\" {\n             format = defaultFormat(rawArgs)\n         }\n         started := false\n         for i := 0; ; i++ {\n             val := first + float64(i)*incr\n             if !inRange(val, incr, last) {\n                 break\n             }\n             if started {\n                 fmt.Fprint(w, opts.separator)\n             }\n             fmt.Fprintf(w, format, val)\n             started = true\n         }\n         if started {\n             fmt.Fprintln(w)\n         }\n     }\n     ```\n     \n     Key existing code for `defaultFormat`:\n     ```go\n     func defaultFormat(rawArgs []string) string {\n         maxPrec := 0\n         for _, arg := range rawArgs {\n             p := argPrecision(arg)\n             if p > maxPrec {\n                 maxPrec = p\n             }\n         }\n         if maxPrec == 0 {\n             return \"%.0f\"\n         }\n         return \"%.\" + strconv.Itoa(maxPrec) + \"f\"\n     }\n     ```\n\n   - **`docs/specs/software-requirements/srd019-seq.yaml`** - Full SRD spec. Key requirements R2.4 (large integers exact up to 2^53), R3.1 (format string validation), R3.2 (format validation errors), R3.3 (equal-width padding), R3.4 (mutual exclusivity of -f and -w).\n\n4. Errors and fixes:\n   - No errors encountered yet. No code has been written or compiled.\n\n5. Problem Solving:\n   - Analyzed existing code to determine what's already implemented vs. what needs to be added\n   - R3.1 (precision preservation) and R3.2 (integer output) appear to be already handled by the existing `defaultFormat()` function\n   - R3.3 (descending sequences) appears to be already handled by `inRange()` \n   - The main missing feature is R2.4: the `-w` equal-width zero-padding logic in `printSequence()`\n   - Need to compute the width of formatted FIRST and LAST, take the maximum, then apply zero-padding\n\n6. All user messages:\n   - First message: Extremely detailed task specification including role definition, repository files list, project context (vision, architecture, specifications, roadmap, analysis, requirement states), execution constitution, go style constitution, task description with requirements/design decisions/acceptance criteria, shared protocols, and package contracts.\n   - Second message: \"CRITICAL: Respond with TEXT ONLY. Do NOT call any tools.\" - requesting a detailed summary of conversation so far.\n\n7. Pending Tasks:\n   - Implement `-w` equal-width zero-padding in `printSequence()` function\n   - Verify R3.1 (precision), R3.2 (integer output), and R3.3 (descending) work correctly with existing code\n   - Run `go vet ./cmd/seq/...` to verify compilation\n   - Run `go build -o bin/seq ./cmd/seq/` to verify binary builds\n   - Test all six acceptance criteria\n\n8. Current Work:\n   I had just finished reading the two key files (`cmd/seq/main.go` and `docs/specs/software-requirements/srd019-seq.yaml`) and was about to plan and implement the changes. The primary work needed is adding equal-width (`-w`) zero-padding logic to `printSequence()`. The approach should be:\n   \n   1. When `opts.equalWidth` is true AND `opts.format` is empty (per D2, -f takes precedence):\n      - Format FIRST and LAST using `defaultFormat(rawArgs)` \n      - Compute string width of each (using `len(fmt.Sprintf(format, first))` and `len(fmt.Sprintf(format, last))`)\n      - Take the maximum width\n      - For integer sequences, use `%0Nd` style or `%0N.0f` style format\n      - For float sequences, use `%0N.Mf` style format where N includes the decimal digits\n\n9. Optional Next Step:\n   Write the modified `cmd/seq/main.go` with equal-width padding support. The task description states: \"Add -w equal-width zero-padding, floating-point precision handling, integer optimization, and negative number support\" to `cmd/seq/main.go`. The specific change needed is modifying `printSequence()` to apply zero-padded formatting when `opts.equalWidth` is true and `opts.format` is empty, computing pad width from the formatted FIRST and LAST values per design decision D1. Then verify with `go vet` and `go build`.\n\nIf you need specific details from before compaction (like exact code snippets, error messages, or content you generated), read the full transcript at: /Users/pdiddy/.claude/projects/-private-var-folders-dl-cbns6zkx7txc7pt64b6cgzp40000gn-T-go-unix-utils-worktrees-5172/4866421a-81c2-4cb7-811e-3a44920d1d6f.jsonl\nContinue the conversation from where it left off without asking the user any further questions. Resume directly — do not acknowledge the summary, do not recap what was happening, do not preface with \"I'll continue\" or similar. Pick up the last task as if the break never happened."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"4866421a-81c2-4cb7-811e-3a44920d1d6f","uuid":"361e55a1-9f20-414c-9340-8a233564aec1","timestamp":"2026-05-14T13:26:11.517Z","isSynthetic":true}
